{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nReplaying a candidate change tells you *what would change*, not whether it is *better*. The store already carries outcome signals needing no LLM/human labeling: revise→re-review pairs (2,483; per-finding survival via `norm_id`), verdict flips on byte-identical material (208; the reviewer-noise floor), bug closes with `close_class=plan_defect` (84), `caused_by` links (89), PASS-then-completion-FAIL tickets (199), reopens (18).\n\n`src/rebar/llm/evals/plan_replay/corpus.py` (celestine-treasonous-ewe, closed) exists: `build_corpus(store_roots, *, cache_dir) -> manifest` recovers PASS/BLOCK/INDETERMINATE `plan_review_result_v1/v2` sidecars via a git-object-walk (`_enumerate_event_blobs` + `_batch_cat`), reconstructs at-review material from CREATE/EDIT/FILE_IMPACT events ONLY (no STATUS/LINK/COMMENT/COMPLETION_VERDICT events), verifies against `material_fingerprint`, and returns `manifest[\"content_hash\"]` — THE corpus identity labels key to. Its per-row cache carries only the summarized sidecar row, not each sidecar's full `data` body (findings array).\n\n`docs/experiments/plan-review-gate/harnesses/mine_outcome_corpus.py` computes `reopen_count`/`force_close`/`completion_verifier_fail_count` as pure functions over a per-ticket event dict (from STATUS/COMMENT/COMPLETION_VERDICT events, via its own git-walk), but its `TRACKER` constant is hard-coded to THIS repo's local `.tickets-tracker` only — its frozen `outcome_corpus.jsonl` artifact has no rows for tickets from other `store_roots` (e.g. legacy-modernization-navigator, 74 rows in the current manifest).\n\n## Approach\n\nBuild `src/rebar/llm/evals/plan_replay/labels.py`:\n\n- **Corpus identity**: call `corpus.build_corpus(store_roots, cache_dir=...)`; `manifest[\"content_hash\"]` is the hash labels key to. `labels.py` refuses (raises, naming both hashes) if a caller-supplied `labels-<hash>.jsonl`'s hash disagrees with a freshly recomputed `build_corpus(...)[\"content_hash\"]`.\n- **Finding-level source**: finding labels need each sidecar's FULL `data` body. Reuse `corpus._enumerate_event_blobs` + `corpus._batch_cat` to re-read the SAME recovered `REVIEW_RESULT` blobs' raw `data` (filtered to `kind == \"REVIEW_RESULT\"`, `data.schema in corpus._SCHEMAS`) — re-projecting the full body, not a second recovery implementation. Before the `norm_id` join, filter each sidecar's `findings` array to `sidecar.surfaced_findings(data)` (`SURFACED_DECISIONS = (\"block\", \"advisory\")`, `sidecar.py:381-394`) — the full `findings` array also carries overflow/indeterminate/permanently-dropped entries, and re-joining an unfiltered population miscounts survival (bug old-frilly-plankton).\n- **Ticket-level event source (multi-store)**: `close_class`/`caused_by` are ordinary ticket-state facts, read via `show_ticket` (compiled `STATUS` state / `deps`) for each corpus ticket id — independent of the REVIEW_RESULT git-walk. `reopen_count`/`force_close`/`completion_verifier_fail_count` reuse `mine_outcome_corpus.py`'s pure functions of that name directly, but `labels.py` supplies their per-ticket event dict from its OWN per-store git-walk (extending `corpus._enumerate_event_blobs`'s content-type filter to also recover STATUS/COMMENT/COMPLETION_VERDICT blobs, mirroring the miner's `_CONTENT_TYPES`) so every `store_roots` entry is covered, not just the local tracker.\n- **Ticket-level labels**: `escaped_defect` = the UNION of (bug ticket has `close_class == \"plan_defect\"`) OR (ticket is the target of an inbound `caused_by` link); `completion_failed_after_pass` (`completion_verifier_fail_count > 0` after a PASS review); `reopened` (`reopen_count > 0`); `clean_close` (closed, no escape signals, closed ≥`CLEAN_CLOSE_MIN_DAYS` days before the corpus build — a named constant, default 7). Each label carries its source event id(s).\n- **Finding-level labels**: for consecutive reviews (surfaced findings only, per above) with changed material, join by `norm_id`; `resolved_by_author` (present in review k, absent in k+1, material changed) vs `persisted`; `noise_flip` for identical-material pairs.\n- **Noise floor**: over identical-material pairs (same `material_fingerprint` across consecutive reviews), compute (a) verdict flip rate, (b) per-criterion finding churn (Jaccard by `norm_id`), and (c) per-question Pass-2 answer agreement from each finding's persisted `verification` field (`sidecar.py:687`, `f.get(\"verification\")` — the binary sub-answer set IS carried on every finding, contra an earlier draft of this ticket).\n- Output `labels-<content_hash>.jsonl` plus a Markdown report with per-label-source counts, joined to `content_hash`.\n\nLabel definitions are constants in one module so a later ticket can tighten them.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/evals/plan_replay/labels.py` (new)\n- `docs/experiments/plan-review-gate/replay/labels-report-<date>.md` (new)\n- `tests/unit/evals/test_plan_replay_labels.py` (new)\n\n## Testing\n\nUnit: synthetic corpus of five tickets exercising each ticket label and each finding label; identical-material pair with a verdict flip counted as noise, changed-material pair not; a ticket with both `plan_defect` and completion FAIL labelled once per source; a permanently-dropped finding excluded from the `norm_id` join. Empty corpus → empty report. A `labels-<hash>.jsonl` built against a stale `content_hash` is refused when re-verified against a rebuilt corpus. A ticket from a non-local store (no miner row) still gets reopen/force_close/completion_verifier_fail counts via the direct git-walk path.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Every review and finding in the corpus carries the labels above, with source event ids; checked by the labels report counts matching independent `jq` counts over the store for `plan_defect`, `caused_by` and reopen events.\n- [ ] The noise floor (verdict flip rate, per-criterion churn, per-question agreement) is published with the identical-material pair count it was computed from; checked in the report.\n- [ ] Labels are keyed to `corpus.build_corpus(...)[\"content_hash\"]` and the loader raises when a fresh recompute disagrees with a supplied file's hash; checked by a unit test.\n- [ ] No LLM calls; checked by running with no provider credentials."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788061540418703321,"uuid":"3facf755-edb0-4153-a375-33b9e044c469"}